// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`the cross-object help surface > the reference mount, verbatim — the only new snapshot entry this feature adds 1`] = `
"### project work-item relation
Usage: pingcode project work-item relation [options] [command]

关联 cross-kind links of a work item (principal_type=work_item)

Options:
  -h, --help                            display help for command

Commands:
  list [options] <ref>                  list the links of one object, filtered by the kind on the
                                        other end
  get [options] <ref> <relation-id>     show one link (the reference is taken for symmetry and not
                                        sent)
  add [options] <ref>                   link this object to another one
  delete [options] <ref> <relation-id>  remove a link — both directions go at once (the reference
                                        is not sent)
  help [command]                        display help for command

accepted --target-type for this principal: idea ticket test_case page
The API declares no vocabulary for this endpoint, so that list is what a live
tenant accepted — it is a hint, not a rule, and nothing is refused locally.
Links between two objects of the same kind are a different, typed family:
reach those with \`pingcode api POST /v1/pjm/work_items/{id}/relations\`.


### project work-item relation list
Usage: pingcode project work-item relation list [options] <ref>

list the links of one object, filtered by the kind on the other end

Arguments:
  ref                   the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept
                        (resolved to an id first)

Options:
  --target-type <kind>  kind of object on the other end — see the note under \`relation --help\` for
                        the pairs this API takes
  --page <n>            page index, 0-based (default: "0")
  --page-size <n>       rows per page, 1-100 (default: "30")
  --all                 walk every page (best effort: the API guarantees no ordering)
  --limit <n>           stop after this many rows with --all (default: "500")
  -h, --help            display help for command

### project work-item relation get
Usage: pingcode project work-item relation get [options] <ref> <relation-id>

show one link (the reference is taken for symmetry and not sent)

Arguments:
  ref          the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept (resolved to
               an id first)
  relation-id  link id, as printed by list

Options:
  -h, --help   display help for command

### project work-item relation add
Usage: pingcode project work-item relation add [options] <ref>

link this object to another one

Arguments:
  ref                   the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept
                        (resolved to an id first)

Options:
  --target-type <kind>  kind of object on the other end — see the note under \`relation --help\` for
                        the pairs this API takes
  --target-id <id>      id of the object on the other end — an id, never a name
  -h, --help            display help for command

### project work-item relation delete
Usage: pingcode project work-item relation delete [options] <ref> <relation-id>

remove a link — both directions go at once (the reference is not sent)

Arguments:
  ref          the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept (resolved to
               an id first)
  relation-id  link id, as printed by list

Options:
  --yes        confirm the deletion — required, and there is no undo on this API
  -h, --help   display help for command

### project work-item comment
Usage: pingcode project work-item comment [options] [command]

评论 comments on a work item (principal_type=work_item)

Options:
  -h, --help                           display help for command

Commands:
  list [options] <ref>                 list the comments on one object, newest first as the API
                                       returns them
  get [options] <ref> <comment-id>     show one comment (the API needs the parent, an id alone is
                                       not enough)
  add [options] <ref>                  comment on this object — the write-back point for an
                                       automated flow
  delete [options] <ref> <comment-id>  soft-delete a comment: the row survives, flagged deleted
  help [command]                       display help for command

delete is a **soft** delete: the row stays in list with STATE=deleted, so treat
that column as the answer to "is it still there". pjm also empties the text;
ship keeps it, so an absent body is not the signal either.


### project work-item comment list
Usage: pingcode project work-item comment list [options] <ref>

list the comments on one object, newest first as the API returns them

Arguments:
  ref              the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept
                   (resolved to an id first)

Options:
  --page <n>       page index, 0-based (default: "0")
  --page-size <n>  rows per page, 1-100 (default: "30")
  --all            walk every page (best effort: the API guarantees no ordering)
  --limit <n>      stop after this many rows with --all (default: "500")
  -h, --help       display help for command

### project work-item comment get
Usage: pingcode project work-item comment get [options] <ref> <comment-id>

show one comment (the API needs the parent, an id alone is not enough)

Arguments:
  ref         the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept (resolved to
              an id first)
  comment-id  comment id, as printed by list

Options:
  -h, --help  display help for command

### project work-item comment add
Usage: pingcode project work-item comment add [options] <ref>

comment on this object — the write-back point for an automated flow

Arguments:
  ref                      the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept
                           (resolved to an id first)

Options:
  --text <text>            comment body, sent verbatim as plain text
  --reply-to <comment-id>  make this a reply to an existing comment on this object
  -h, --help               display help for command

### project work-item comment delete
Usage: pingcode project work-item comment delete [options] <ref> <comment-id>

soft-delete a comment: the row survives, flagged deleted

Arguments:
  ref         the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept (resolved to
              an id first)
  comment-id  comment id, as printed by list

Options:
  --yes       confirm the deletion — required, and there is no undo on this API
  -h, --help  display help for command

### project work-item attachment
Usage: pingcode project work-item attachment [options] [command]

附件 attachments of a work item (principal_type=work_item)

Options:
  -h, --help                              display help for command

Commands:
  list [options] <ref>                    list attachments — without --comment-id only the
                                          object-level files
  get [options] <ref> <attachment-id>     show one attachment — a snippet needs the --comment-id it
                                          lives under
  add-snippet [options] <ref>             attach a code snippet to a comment (the only attachment
                                          write there is)
  delete [options] <ref> <attachment-id>  delete an attachment for good — no soft delete, no undo
  help [command]                          display help for command

There is no file upload here: the API takes a file as one-step multipart/form-data,
which this CLI cannot send, so only code snippets can be written. Snippets always
hang off a comment — create one first and pass its id as --comment-id, on write
and on every read, or the snippet is reported as not found.


### project work-item attachment list
Usage: pingcode project work-item attachment list [options] <ref>

list attachments — without --comment-id only the object-level files

Arguments:
  ref                the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept
                     (resolved to an id first)

Options:
  --comment-id <id>  scope to one comment — mandatory for a snippet, which is invisible without it
  --page <n>         page index, 0-based (default: "0")
  --page-size <n>    rows per page, 1-100 (default: "30")
  --all              walk every page (best effort: the API guarantees no ordering)
  --limit <n>        stop after this many rows with --all (default: "500")
  -h, --help         display help for command

### project work-item attachment get
Usage: pingcode project work-item attachment get [options] <ref> <attachment-id>

show one attachment — a snippet needs the --comment-id it lives under

Arguments:
  ref                the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept
                     (resolved to an id first)
  attachment-id      attachment id, as printed by list

Options:
  --comment-id <id>  scope to one comment — mandatory for a snippet, which is invisible without it
  -h, --help         display help for command

### project work-item attachment add-snippet
Usage: pingcode project work-item attachment add-snippet [options] <ref>

attach a code snippet to a comment (the only attachment write there is)

Arguments:
  ref                    the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept
                         (resolved to an id first)

Options:
  --comment-id <id>      comment to hang it on — required by the API in practice
  --title <text>         snippet title, shown in the UI
  --format <language>    language, one of: clike css dart django dockerfile go markdown nginx
                         python php shell sql swift html javascript jsx pascal sass stylus vue yaml
                         haskell
  --content <text>       snippet body, sent verbatim
  --content-file <path>  snippet body, read from a file instead of --content
  -h, --help             display help for command

### project work-item attachment delete
Usage: pingcode project work-item attachment delete [options] <ref> <attachment-id>

delete an attachment for good — no soft delete, no undo

Arguments:
  ref                the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept
                     (resolved to an id first)
  attachment-id      attachment id, as printed by list

Options:
  --comment-id <id>  scope to one comment — mandatory for a snippet, which is invisible without it
  --yes              confirm the deletion — required, and there is no undo on this API
  -h, --help         display help for command

### project work-item activity
Usage: pingcode project work-item activity [options] [command]

活动记录 audit trail of a work item (principal_type=work_item)

Options:
  -h, --help                         display help for command

Commands:
  list [options] <ref>               list what happened to one object, as the API orders it
  get [options] <ref> <activity-id>  show one audit row, with its free-form content object
  help [command]                     display help for command

Read-only, and per-object: this API has no webhooks and no global change feed,
so polling one object here is the only change stream there is.
EVENT is the machine-readable name; SUMMARY is Chinese prose and not a contract.


### project work-item activity list
Usage: pingcode project work-item activity list [options] <ref>

list what happened to one object, as the API orders it

Arguments:
  ref              the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept
                   (resolved to an id first)

Options:
  --page <n>       page index, 0-based (default: "0")
  --page-size <n>  rows per page, 1-100 (default: "30")
  --all            walk every page (best effort: the API guarantees no ordering)
  --limit <n>      stop after this many rows with --all (default: "500")
  -h, --help       display help for command

### project work-item activity get
Usage: pingcode project work-item activity get [options] <ref> <activity-id>

show one audit row, with its free-form content object

Arguments:
  ref          the work item this applies to, in any form its own read commands accept (resolved to
               an id first)
  activity-id  activity id, as printed by list

Options:
  -h, --help   display help for command
"
`;
